    <article class="reader-panel heritage-dossier" id="shivaratri">
      <p class="eyebrow">01 / THE NIGHT OF SHIVA</p><h2>Maha Shivaratri</h2><figure class="journal-illustration"><img src="/assets/images/shivaratri-vigil-journal.webp" alt="Illustrated stone Shiva linga with bilva leaves, flowers and glowing brass lamps in a quiet shrine at night" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>A quiet night of devotion · AI-generated devotional illustration; an imagined shrine.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Maha Shivaratri honours Shiva through worship, prayer and, in many communities, a night vigil. Temple observances may include abhisheka, the ceremonial bathing of the Shiva linga.</p>
      <h3>More than one sacred association</h3>
      <p>Devotional explanations connect the festival with Shiva and Parvati’s union, Shiva’s cosmic dance, and his protection of the worlds from Halahala. These are different associations, rather than one continuous episode.</p>
      <h3>From stillness to movement</h3>
      <p>Forms of celebration differ by place. At Chidambaram, Natyanjali offers worship through dance; other Shiva centres draw pilgrims for temple worship and prayer. A quiet vigil and a dance offering can express devotion in different ways.</p>
      <p class="editorial-reflection"><strong>A question to carry:</strong> How does the Halahala story connect strength with compassion? This is an editorial invitation to reflection.</p>
      <div class="journal-links"><a href="/shiva-halahala.html">Read Shiva drinks Halahala</a><a href="/shiva-parvati.html">Read Shiva and Parvati</a><a href="/deities.html#shiva">Meet Shiva</a></div>
      <section class="journal-reading-route" aria-labelledby="shivaratri-telling"><p class="eyebrow">A TELLING FROM THE SUPPLIED BOOKS</p><h3 id="shivaratri-telling">An unexpected act of worship</h3><p>In the Skanda Purana’s Kedara-khanda, a forester named Canda spends a night awake in a bilva tree. Leaves and water fall onto a Shiva linga below, becoming an offering he has not consciously intended. Later, when a dog eats the food brought for him, his response changes from anger toward acceptance. The account connects Shivaratri with transformation and Shiva’s grace.</p><p>This is one textual telling, distinct from the festival’s other sacred associations.</p><a href="/sources.html#supplied-journal-books">Source: Kedara-khanda 33.9–15, 29–45 →</a></section>
      <p class="collection-source"><a href="/sources.html#festivals">Festival sources &amp; context</a></p>
    </article>
    <article class="reader-panel heritage-dossier" id="navaratri">
      <p class="eyebrow">02 / NINE NIGHTS</p><h2>Navaratri</h2><figure class="journal-illustration"><img src="/assets/images/navaratri-garba-journal.webp" alt="Illustrated Gujarati garba gathering with dancers circling a lit clay garbo in a courtyard at dusk" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Garba during Navaratri · AI-generated cultural illustration.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Navaratri means “nine nights.” Devotion to the Goddess finds expression through worship, stories, music and community gatherings. Different regions give the celebration distinct forms.</p>
      <h3>A tradition with many local voices</h3>
      <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>Gujarat</dt><dd>Garba brings people together in circular dance and devotional celebration.</dd></div><div><dt>West Bengal</dt><dd>Durga Puja centres on the Goddess through elaborate images, worship and public celebration.</dd></div><div><dt>Tamil Nadu</dt><dd>Golu displays arrange dolls and figurines as part of the festival’s storytelling and devotional world.</dd></div></dl>
      <p>These examples introduce a wider diversity; they do not describe every household or community. The Devi and Mahishasura narrative offers one doorway into the festival’s theme of divine protection.</p>
      <p class="editorial-reflection"><strong>A question to carry:</strong> What can a story, a dance and a carefully arranged display each help us remember?</p>
      <div class="journal-links"><a href="/devi-mahishasura.html">Read Devi and Mahishasura</a><a href="/deities.html#devi">Explore the Goddess</a></div>
