    </article>
    <article class="reader-panel heritage-dossier" id="hanuman-jayanti">
      <p class="eyebrow">03 / DEVOTION IN ACTION</p><h2>Hanuman Jayanti</h2><figure class="journal-illustration"><img src="/assets/images/hanuman-jayanti-reading-journal.webp" alt="Illustrated multigenerational gathering reading a devotional book together in a sunlit temple courtyard" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Reading in community · AI-generated cultural illustration; an imagined gathering.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Hanuman Jayanti celebrates Hanuman’s birth and honours his devotion. The Sundara Kanda, which tells of his search for Sita, also has a place in festival observances: Tirumala Tirupati Devasthanams has organised its recitation as part of Hanuman Jayanti festivities.</p>
      <h3>Honouring a shared figure in different ways</h3>
      <p>Festival timing follows different regional calendars and temple traditions. Sri Venkateswara Temple’s account distinguishes Tamil Nadu and Kerala observances from those in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana. Local temple practice offers the appropriate context for a community’s celebration.</p>
      <p>In Hanuman’s ocean crossing, courage works alongside courtesy and good judgment. His mission gives his strength a purpose: finding Sita in service of Rama.</p>
      <p class="editorial-reflection"><strong>A question to carry:</strong> When does careful thinking help courage become useful?</p>
